WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.509 --> 00:00:03.776
- [Narrator] Just across the
border from war-torn Syria,

00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:06.360
in a forgotten city is
an unexpected sight:

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.860
the Hotel Palmyra.

00:00:08.744 --> 00:00:11.582
(chiming music)

00:00:11.582 --> 00:00:13.741
- This hotel is different
than others, uh, because

00:00:13.741 --> 00:00:15.452
time has washed away the walls,

00:00:15.452 --> 00:00:17.206
time has left this impact.

00:00:17.206 --> 00:00:20.418
It is, it is a journey into the past.

00:00:20.418 --> 00:00:22.968
So many people has passed
through this hotel.

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:25.926
(elegant music)

00:00:25.926 --> 00:00:29.297
(simple piano music)

00:00:29.297 --> 00:00:32.297
This hotel has been here since 1874.

00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:36.812
And this is why we try
to keep it as it is.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.766
The city of Baalbek
has changed, of course.

00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:42.262
It's feeling the impact of the war

00:00:42.262 --> 00:00:43.875
on one side of the borders,

00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:45.316
but it's also feeling the effects of

00:00:45.316 --> 00:00:49.856
economic depression that's
affecting the whole country.

00:00:49.856 --> 00:00:52.318
At one point, there were
no visitors to speak of,

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.081
and that was very difficult.

00:00:55.081 --> 00:00:57.815
(bright, lively music)

00:00:57.815 --> 00:00:59.805
- [Narrator] The hotel used
to be a haven for visitors

00:00:59.805 --> 00:01:03.757
because of the ancient Roman
ruins just outside its doors.

00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:04.860
- [Rima] It is a constant reminder

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.432
of the importance of Baalbek.

00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:11.584
And this is something that was
not accidentally built here.

00:01:11.584 --> 00:01:14.750
If I wanted to describe the
view in front of me daily,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.302
when I look out of the window,
when I come to the hotel,

00:01:17.302 --> 00:01:18.656
I'd be speechless.

00:01:18.656 --> 00:01:22.547
And generally, it's hard
for me to be speechless.

00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:24.720
(upbeat music)

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.842
The smell of this hotel is of carpets,

00:01:27.842 --> 00:01:30.592
old walls, and uh, rusty faucets,

00:01:32.498 --> 00:01:35.265
but that makes me smile.

00:01:35.265 --> 00:01:36.806
And this is, I think, essentially

00:01:36.806 --> 00:01:39.139
what memories are all about.

00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:43.096
The old personnel that has
been here since the '50s

00:01:43.096 --> 00:01:45.936
and some, from the '60s,
have stayed with us

00:01:45.936 --> 00:01:48.737
because, to them, it's home.

00:02:05.238 --> 00:02:07.315
- [Narrator] And this is the
feeling that keeps them going,

00:02:07.315 --> 00:02:10.935
even with tourism
dwindling in this region.

00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:13.569
- [Rima] We have never closed
the doors of the hotel.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:16.392
No one has a right to touch Hotel Palmyra,

00:02:16.392 --> 00:02:17.725
except for time.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.473
(percussive chiming music)

00:02:24.152 --> 00:02:26.902
(soothing chord)

